# 6. Results

All figures are on the frozen test block {{test_start}} to {{test_end}}, five seeds, with
Task N and Task F reported separately throughout.

## 6.1 Task N — leave-city-out nowcasting

| Model | RMSE (µg/m³) | MAE | R² |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| best spatial baseline (kriging) | {{p3n_ordinary_kriging_rmse}} | — | {{p3n_ordinary_kriging_r2}} |
| training-pool mean (constant) | {{p3n_training_pool_mean_rmse}} | — | {{p3n_training_pool_mean_r2}} |
| CAMS, pooled debias | {{taskn_cams_rmse}} | {{taskn_cams_mae}} | {{taskn_cams_r2}} |
| LightGBM, static only | {{taskn_static_only_rmse}} | {{taskn_static_only_mae}} | {{taskn_static_only_r2}} |
| LightGBM, deployable | {{taskn_deployable_rmse}} | {{taskn_deployable_mae}} | {{taskn_deployable_r2}} |
| LightGBM, retrospective | {{taskn_retrospective_rmse}} | {{taskn_retrospective_mae}} | {{taskn_retrospective_r2}} |

**The learned model beats every baseline, and its R² is {{taskn_retrospective_r2}}.** Both
statements are load-bearing. It improves on pooled-debiased CAMS by
{{taskn_cams_rmse}} → {{taskn_retrospective_rmse}} µg/m³ and on the best spatial
interpolator by a wider margin. It also explains almost none of the variance in a
never-seen city. We report this as the result: predicting urban PM2.5 at a location with no
local monitor, from a training set of five other cities, is close to unsolved in this
region, and the benchmark exists to make that measurable rather than to conceal it.

## 6.2 Per-city results and the Diebold–Mariano tests

Pooling six cities into one number hides the finding.

| Held-out city | LightGBM RMSE | CAMS RMSE | DM statistic | *p* |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Almaty | {{rmse_lgbm_almaty}} | {{rmse_cams_almaty}} | {{dm_almaty_stat}} | {{dm_almaty_p}} |
| Ashgabat | {{rmse_lgbm_ashgabat}} | {{rmse_cams_ashgabat}} | {{dm_ashgabat_stat}} | {{dm_ashgabat_p}} |
| Bishkek | {{rmse_lgbm_bishkek}} | {{rmse_cams_bishkek}} | {{dm_bishkek_stat}} | {{dm_bishkek_p}} |
| Dushanbe | {{rmse_lgbm_dushanbe}} | {{rmse_cams_dushanbe}} | {{dm_dushanbe_stat}} | {{dm_dushanbe_p}} |
| Khujand (zero-shot) | {{rmse_lgbm_khujand}} | {{rmse_cams_khujand}} | {{dm_khujand_stat}} | {{dm_khujand_p}} |
| Tashkent | {{rmse_lgbm_tashkent}} | {{rmse_cams_tashkent}} | {{dm_tashkent_stat}} | {{dm_tashkent_p}} |
| **pooled** (n = {{dm_pooled_n}}) | **{{rmse_lgbm_pooled}}** | **{{rmse_cams_pooled}}** | **{{dm_pooled_stat}}** | **{{dm_pooled_p}}** |

Tests use Newey–West HAC variance with the Harvey–Leybourne–Newbold small-sample
correction. Negative statistics favour the learned model.

**The pooled improvement is significant ({{dm_pooled_p}}); only {{dm_n_sig}} of
{{dm_n_folds}} individual folds are.** Bishkek ({{dm_bishkek_p}}) and Tashkent
({{dm_tashkent_p}}) show lower RMSE that does not clear significance, and we do not describe
those as improvements. **{{dm_fold_favouring_cams}} favours CAMS outright**
({{rmse_lgbm_ashgabat}} vs {{rmse_cams_ashgabat}} µg/m³, DM statistic
{{dm_ashgabat_stat}}, *p* = {{dm_ashgabat_p}}) — the sign is reversed and the result is far
from significant, so the honest summary is that the two are indistinguishable there rather
than that CAMS wins.

**Khujand, the zero-shot fold, is significant ({{dm_khujand_p}})** at
{{rmse_lgbm_khujand}} µg/m³ against CAMS at {{rmse_cams_khujand}}. A city with no training
rows at all is predicted better than by the operational chemistry-transport model. It
remains among the hardest folds in absolute terms, second only to Dushanbe.

**Truncation-lag sensitivity.** The DM truncation lag is derived from the forecast horizon,
and an early implementation passed `horizon_hours = 1` for the nowcasting task, which
disables the HAC correction entirely and inflated *p*-values by roughly seven orders of
magnitude. We now report the full sensitivity sweep: across truncation lags of
{{dm_lag_range}} hours the pooled conclusion is stable, with all comparisons significant
({{dm_lag_all_sig}}) and a worst-case *p* of {{dm_lag_min_p}}.

**Figure 3** gives the per-city comparison against debiased CAMS, and **Figure 5** the
observed-versus-predicted scatter with the 1:1 line. The compression toward the mean
visible in Figure 5 is the same effect that drives the RMSE/exceedance divergence of
Section 4.3.

![Figure 3](figures/fig3_per_city_rmse.png)

