| LightGBM, retrospective | {{taskf_retrospective_rmse}} | {{taskf_retrospective_r2}} |

Task F is the easier problem and the numbers say so: R² = {{taskf_retrospective_r2}} against
{{taskn_retrospective_r2}} for Task N. **These two figures must never be quoted together as
though they described one system.** The Task F model may read the station's own recent
history; the Task N model is predicting a city it has never seen. Reporting
{{taskf_retrospective_rmse}} µg/m³ as "the model's accuracy" would describe a capability the
deployment does not have at unmonitored locations, which is the case the artefact exists to
serve.

## 6.4 SHAP attribution: what actually carries the model

Attribution uses SHAP values (Lundberg and Lee, 2017), computed on the tuned model over
the held-out folds.

Mean absolute SHAP over the test block, by feature family:

| Family | Share of total attribution |
|---|---:|
| spatial neighbour | **{{shap_spatial_neighbour_pct}}%** |
| static geography | {{shap_static_geography_pct}}% |
| calendar | {{shap_calendar_pct}}% |
| satellite | {{shap_satellite_pct}}% |
| CAMS forecast | {{shap_cams_forecast_pct}}% |
| satellite missingness | {{shap_satellite_missingness_pct}}% |

**Spatial interpolation drives the model, not the satellite record.** The single largest
feature is `{{shap_top_feature}}` at {{shap_top_value}} mean absolute SHAP — inverse-distance
weighted neighbour concentration — more than twice the second-ranked feature
(`{{shap_second_feature}}`, {{shap_second_value}}). Spatial neighbours and static geography
together account for
{{shap_spatial_neighbour_pct}}% + {{shap_static_geography_pct}}% of attribution, while the
five satellite products contribute {{shap_satellite_pct}}% between them.

This is the paper's least comfortable result and we state it plainly: **the model is largely
a well-tuned spatial interpolator with geographic priors.** The satellite features are not
inert — {{shap_satellite_pct}}% is not nothing, and Section 5.5 shows the deployable set
loses little — but a reader would be entitled to expect, from a study built on five
remote-sensing products, that those products carried the prediction. They do not.

Two further readings follow. First, **satellite missingness earns
{{shap_satellite_missingness_pct}}% — comparable to the {{shap_cams_forecast_pct}}%
contributed by the entire chemistry-transport model.** Whether a retrieval failed carries
nearly as much information as what the atmospheric model predicted, which vindicates
modelling missingness rather than dropping it (Section 2.4) and simultaneously indicates how
little the retrieved values themselves add. Second, calendar features at
{{shap_calendar_pct}}% confirm that a large part of the signal is seasonal regularity that
any climatology captures — consistent with the same-hour 7-day mean being the only Task F
baseline with positive R².

**Figure 4** shows attribution by feature family.

![Figure 4](figures/fig4_shap_by_family.png)

**Figure 4.** Feature attribution by family, as a share of total mean |SHAP|. Satellite
families are hatched. Spatial neighbour features dominate; the five satellite products
together contribute less than static geography.

## 6.5 Summary of what is and is not established

Established:

- The learned model beats pooled-debiased CAMS across the {{n_loco_folds}}-fold leave-city-out
  protocol, pooled *p* = {{dm_pooled_p}}.
- Deployability costs almost nothing: {{taskn_deployable_rmse}} vs
  {{taskn_retrospective_rmse}} µg/m³.
- Zero-shot transfer to a city with no training rows beats CAMS significantly
  ({{dm_khujand_p}}).

Not established:

- Per-city superiority. {{dm_n_sig}} of {{dm_n_folds}} folds reach significance;
  {{dm_fold_favouring_cams}} does not favour the model at all.
- Useful absolute accuracy at unmonitored locations. R² = {{taskn_retrospective_r2}}.
- That satellite remote sensing drives the result. Section 6.4 shows it does not.
